@page "/Projects/Portfolio"

<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/Portfolio" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <h3>Project description</h3>
    <span>
      I built a Blazor site to display the projects i'm proud of. But one of the project I'm proud of is this portfolio site itself.
      As we only learnt unity and unreal at my study it was a real challenge because I didn't really know basic web development things.
      Like how a DOM works for example or routing on the client vs routing on server.
    </span>
    <h3>How the start of the project went</h3>
    <span>
      At the beginning of the project I was advised by my one of my classmates to build my portfolio site with React.
      So with little Javascript knowledge I started off with React.
      It was very daunting but I managed to get a basic portfolio site going.
      After I took a break however I forgot most of what I made and when I read through the Html/Javascript blend,
      I just thought: "surely something with more comprehensible syntax must exist".
      I had already heard of Blazor before so I decided to give it a try.
    </span>
    <h3>The start of the Blazor project</h3>
    <span>
      After transferring some HTML and CSS code from my React project I got a basic Blazor site.
      I didn't have to learn that much new syntax and just looked at the template code whenever I didn't know how to do something.
      Syntax wise Blazor felt like a breeze compared to React.
      The organization was now way better as I already knew from the React page what layout I wanted.
      So I adjusted my Blazor project accordingly.
      What I enjoyed the most about Blazor was the routing system en the built-in scoped css.
      What took me the most time however was figuring out what the wwwroot was. Or what a static page meant in the first place.
      Pretty silly looking back now. But also not that weird that I didn't understand looking back now. 
      I'd only ever used .NET itself so far. And only in game programming context.
      Oh well atleast I know now.
    </span>
    <h3>My hosting and domain name adventures</h3>
    <span>
      As GitHub pages was my first choice for hosting I had to figure out how to host a Blazor site on GitHub pages.
      Luckily I quickly found out about the existence of Jekyll. Jekyll was able to build en deploy the Blazor site to GitHub pages.
      Very nice for me as I just pushed to the main branch on GitHub and Jekyll took care of the rest.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

}
